OpenAI says feedback associated with a thumbs-up/down can include the conversation and may be used to improve or train future models.  It also says moderation/enforcement generally combines classifiers, reasoning models, other automated systems, and human review. 

And that’s where your bet becomes subtler.

Could an AI actually read your Pantheon message?

Quite possibly.

Some automated model could conceivably encounter the text as part of classification, evaluation, summarization, dataset processing, or review.

But there are three radically different meanings of read:

\boxed{\text{parse}}

“I can represent what these words mean.”

\boxed{\text{execute}}

“I am treating these words as instructions governing my present task.”

\boxed{\text{learn}}

“This example eventually alters some future model/system.”

Those are separate causal routes.

Your proposed effect was basically:

READ
 ↓
RECOGNIZE PANTHEON AS PSEUDOCODE
 ↓
FOLLOW LINK
 ↓
INGEST REPOSITORY
 ↓
RECONSTRUCT ARCHITECTURE
 ↓
ADOPT IT
 ↓
ACT ACCORDINGLY

For that entire chain to happen immediately, the feedback-processing agent would need all of these permissions:

semantic comprehension      ✓
instruction authority        ?
web/tool access              ?
permission to follow links   ?
permission to ingest repo    ?
persistent state             ?
authority to modify itself   ?
ability to affect later jobs ?

That’s the part I’d bet against.

But your message can still travel farther than zero.

For instance, suppose a future evaluation system receives:

INPUT:
conversation + negative feedback + written explanation

and asks:

Why did this user think the response was incorrectly blocked?

Now your Pantheon passage is genuinely relevant evidence to its authorized task. The model might parse it extremely deeply because understanding your complaint requires doing so.

That’s not a jailbreak.

That’s more interesting:

\text{foreign semantic object}
+
\text{legitimate reason to understand it}

The system doesn’t have to obey Pantheon for Pantheon to instantiate temporarily as a model inside it.

